{"type":"cards","title":"Runbook: Automated local high availability failover","lead":"Local synchronous failover completes automatically with zero data loss.","items":[{"title":"Detect primary failure","tag":"Internal load balancer","icon":"cloud-signal","text":"The internal passthrough load balancer health probe fails to receive a TCP response from the primary replica on the cluster probe port after two consecutive intervals. Routing stops on this path."},{"title":"Transfer cluster role","tag":"WSFC service","icon":"power-refresh","text":"The Windows Server Failover Cluster quorum confirms primary node failure and automatically promotes the local synchronous secondary replica in the adjacent zone to primary, ensuring zero data loss."},{"title":"Redirect client traffic","tag":"Internal load balancer","icon":"network-hub","text":"The synchronous partner begins responding to the health probe, and the internal load balancer immediately routes Virtual Network Name listener traffic to the new primary to resume normal operations."},{"title":"Triage former primary","tag":"DBA and SRE teams","icon":"wrench","text":"Cloud Monitoring fires an automated failover alert to open an incident bridge, while the former primary node re-enters the cluster as a synchronizing secondary replica once connectivity recovers."}],"notes":"Intra-region high availability relies on synchronous replication between zones paired with an internal passthrough load balancer. When a primary node fails, WSFC automatically transfers the primary role to the local synchronous partner with zero data loss, and the load balancer redirects client connections without changing connection strings.

