{"type":"process","design":"S66_STEP_COLUMNS","title":"Runbook: Zero-downtime OS and SQL patching","items":[{"title":"Patch DR replica","tag":"Cloud and DBA teams","icon":"cloud-download","text":"Apply Windows OS updates and SQL Server Cumulative Updates to the asynchronous disaster recovery replica first, rebooting and validating replication health before proceeding."},{"title":"Patch local secondary","tag":"Cloud and DBA teams","icon":"gear-download","text":"Drain read-only reporting connections from the primary region synchronous secondary node, apply patches, reboot the VM, and wait for Availability Group synchronization."},{"title":"Trigger planned failover","tag":"DBA team","icon":"power-refresh","text":"Verify zero replication lag between the local primary and patched synchronous secondary, then initiate a planned manual failover to promote the patched secondary node."},{"title":"Patch former primary","tag":"Cloud and DBA teams","icon":"shield-gear","text":"Apply the identical OS and SQL Server patch baseline to the former primary node, reboot, confirm cluster health, and verify synchronous replication status across zones."}],"notes":"Rolling patching eliminates unplanned reboot outages by always updating secondary replicas first. By patching the DR node, then the local synchronous secondary, executing a planned zero-data-loss failover, and finally patching the former primary, Contoso Health maintains continuous database availability.

